              'description': 'Beyond the classic autosomal dominant/recessive patterns, X-linked inheritance and de novo (newly arising, non-inherited) mutations add further complexity to how a genetic disease is transmitted or arises.',
              'mechanism': 'Autosomal dominant and recessive follow classic Mendelian patterns. Y-linked inheritance is straightforward (father to son only). X-linked "dominant" and "recessive" patterns are complicated by X-inactivation and mosaicism — a female carrier’s symptom severity can vary because different cells randomly inactivate different X chromosomes ("skewed X-inactivation" can shift severity). A de novo mutation arises newly in an individual (not inherited from either parent), while somatic mosaicism means only a subset of the body’s cells carry a mutation, arising after fertilisation.',
