              'description': 'Retroviruses genuinely integrate into the host genome — a powerful feature for permanent gene correction, but one that caused a serious real-world safety disaster before lentivirus offered a safer alternative.',
              'mechanism': 'Unlike AAV and adenovirus (DNA viruses that don’t meaningfully integrate), retroviruses are RNA viruses that use reverse transcriptase to convert their RNA genome into DNA, which then genuinely integrates into the host genome — this is retroviruses’ defining feature. Early gammaretroviral vectors (e.g. based on murine leukemia virus) successfully treated X-linked severe combined immunodeficiency (X-SCID) by modifying patients’ hematopoietic stem cells ex vivo — but caused paediatric leukemia in several patients, because gammaretroviruses tend to integrate near proto-oncogenes, and insertional overexpression of a nearby proto-oncogene drove uncontrolled cell division. Lentivirus (HIV-derived) is now used instead: it still integrates (retaining permanence), but with a different, safer integration-site profile, and — unlike gammaretrovirus — can transduce non-dividing cells too. Lentivirus is only used ex vivo, and is produced by a similar triple-plasmid approach to AAV.',
              'funFacts': [],
              'activeRecall': ['Why did the original gammaretroviral X-SCID gene therapy cause leukemia, mechanistically?', 'What two advantages does lentivirus have over the gammaretrovirus it replaced?'],
