              'images': [],
              'description': 'CRISPR-Cas9 technology has moved beyond simply cutting DNA, toward directly rewriting individual bases — with the first clinically approved therapy already in use.',
              'mechanism': 'Casgevy (exagamglogene autotemcel) is the first clinically approved CRISPR-Cas9 therapy, used ex vivo to treat severe sickle cell disease. Because it’s ex vivo, no viral packaging is needed — a ribonucleoprotein (guide RNA plus Cas9 protein) is simply mixed together and delivered into cells by electroporation. Casgevy disrupts an enhancer for a transcription factor that normally silences fetal haemoglobin, allowing fetal haemoglobin production to resume — functionally curative. Base editing goes further than cutting: Cas9 is modified into a nickase (nCas9, creating only a single-strand break, safer than a double-strand break) fused to a deaminase enzyme, which directly chemically converts one base to another (A↔G or C↔T "transitions" are currently feasible; "transversions", changing the number of rings in the base, are harder). Beam Therapeutics is currently trialling base editing (packaged in lipid nanoparticles, targeting the liver) for glycogen storage disease type 1, targeting only the most common causative mutation. Prime editing goes further still: nCas9 is fused to a reverse transcriptase, and the guide RNA is replaced with a prime editing guide (PEG) RNA that directly specifies a new sequence to write in — expected to enter its first trials in 2026, again via liver-targeted LNP delivery.',
