              'mechanism': 'Christianson syndrome causes severe intellectual disability, ataxia, epilepsy, and autism, following an X-linked "recessive" pattern complicated by skewed X-inactivation. Mutation types span the full spectrum (missense, nonsense, frame-shifting, deletion, insertion, indels, splice-site), mostly causing loss of function of the NHE6 protein. The shaker rat, which carries a loss-of-function Slc9a6 mutation, models the ataxic phenotype and reduced NHE6 protein seen in affected patients. AAV-delivered SLC9A6 gene replacement measurably improves motor performance (straighter gait, lower ataxia score) and reduces cerebellar degeneration in the shaker rat model — remarkably, with only a small, partial restoration of NHE6 protein levels. Timing of treatment is critical: AAV given at postnatal day 4 was about 99.5% effective at preventing the ataxic phenotype, versus roughly 38% effective at 7 weeks and only about 18% effective at 12 weeks — showing a rapidly closing treatment window as disease progresses.',
