              'images': [],
              'description': 'Affinity is how strongly a drug binds its receptor (measured as Kd); efficacy (intrinsic activity) is its ability to activate the receptor once bound. Full agonists have intrinsic activity 1, partial agonists between 0 and 1, and antagonists 0.',
              'mechanism': 'Affinity is expressed as the equilibrium dissociation constant Kd (mol/L), so higher affinity means a lower Kd. In Van Rossum\'s substituted methonium compounds, enlarging the extra chemical group lowered the maximum response, showing falling efficacy. Inverse agonists act on receptors with activity in the absence of ligand (e.g. β2 and H1 receptors). Specificity is how far a drug recognises a single target; it falls as concentration rises and generally rises with affinity. Selectivity is the ability of a dose to produce one effect rather than another, measured as the potency difference between receptor types (e.g. salbutamol for β2 and atenolol for β1).',
