              'images': [],
              'description': 'Schild analysis uses agonist dose ratios measured at several antagonist concentrations to test whether antagonism is competitive and to estimate the antagonist\'s dissociation constant (KB) and pA2.',
              'mechanism': 'The dose ratio (rA) is the agonist EC50 with antagonist divided by the EC50 without it (e.g. 9 × 10⁻⁹ / 1.5 × 10⁻⁹ = 6). Plotting log(rA − 1) against log antagonist concentration (xB) gives a straight line, log(rA − 1) = log xB − log KB, with one point per antagonist concentration. For competitive antagonism the slope is 1, and the x-intercept equals −pA2, where pA2 = pKB (e.g. x-intercept −8.66 gives KB ≈ 2.2 × 10⁻⁹ M). pA2 is the negative log molar antagonist concentration that gives a dose ratio of 2 (twice the agonist needed). A slope other than 1 means the antagonism is not competitive. KB depends on the antagonist and receptor, not on agonist affinity or efficacy, so it is useful for comparing antagonists and identifying receptors.',
              'funFacts': [],
              'notes': [],
              'activeRecall': ['What does a Schild slope different from 1 tell you?', 'Define pA2 in words.'],
              'saq': [],
              'refs': [],
              'hashtags': ['Pharmacodynamics', 'Antagonists'],
              'course': 'PCOL2021',
