              'mechanism': 'Non-competitive antagonism (e.g. blocking a downstream mechanism) lowers the maximum response, and extra agonist cannot overcome it; the Schild equations do not apply. Physiological antagonism describes opposing effects through different receptors. In the heart, noradrenaline acts on β1-adrenoceptors (Gs) to stimulate adenylyl cyclase, raise cAMP and increase heart rate, while ACh acts on M2 receptors (Gi) to inhibit the same enzyme. In the airway, ACh (M3), histamine (H1) and leukotrienes act through Gq–PLC–IP3/DAG to raise Ca²⁺ and contract smooth muscle, while adrenaline at β2-adrenoceptors (Gs, cAMP) relaxes it. Atropine (muscarinic) and atenolol (β1) are used to test parasympathetic and sympathetic input to heart rate.',
