              'label': 'Sympathetic vs parasympathetic organisation',
              'images': [],
              'description': 'The autonomic nervous system has parasympathetic, sympathetic and enteric divisions that control functions largely outside voluntary control. The parasympathetic and sympathetic divisions differ in where they leave the CNS and where their ganglia sit.',
              'mechanism': 'Parasympathetic nerves leave the CNS as a craniosacral outflow: cranial nerves III, VII, IX and X (the vagus, which supplies the heart, lungs and upper GI tract) plus sacral segments S2 to S4. Sympathetic nerves form a thoracolumbar outflow from T1 to T12 and L1 to L2. Each pathway runs preganglionic fibre, ganglion, postganglionic fibre: parasympathetic fibres have long preganglionic and short postganglionic fibres with ganglia on or near the organ, while sympathetic fibres have short preganglionic and long postganglionic fibres with ganglia mostly in the sympathetic chain beside the spinal cord. Organs with dual innervation, such as the heart, usually receive opposing effects (parasympathetic bradycardia, sympathetic tachycardia), while some receive only one: the ciliary muscle is parasympathetic only, and sweat glands and most blood vessels are sympathetic only. The enteric nervous system of the gut plexuses can run local reflexes independently but is closely interconnected with both divisions.',
