              'description': 'Acetylcholine and noradrenaline are the two main autonomic transmitters. Which one a fibre releases depends on whether it is preganglionic or postganglionic and which division it belongs to.',
              'mechanism': 'All preganglionic autonomic fibres, parasympathetic and sympathetic, release acetylcholine, as do all postganglionic parasympathetic fibres. Most postganglionic sympathetic fibres release noradrenaline; the exception is the sympathetic supply to sweat glands, which releases acetylcholine, and the adrenal medulla, which is supplied by sympathetic preganglionic fibres and releases adrenaline and noradrenaline. Acetylcholine is made by choline acetyltransferase from acetyl-CoA and choline, and is broken down by acetylcholinesterase to choline and acetic acid; the choline is taken back up and reused. Noradrenaline is synthesised from tyrosine (tyrosine to DOPA to dopamine to noradrenaline, then adrenaline). About 75% of released noradrenaline is taken back up by the norepinephrine transporter (NET), the rest is broken down by MAO and COMT, and noradrenaline also acts on presynaptic α2 receptors to regulate its own release.',
