              'mechanism': 'Glycinergic interneurons in the spinal cord gate pain signals to the brain, and this inhibition is reduced in neuropathic pain; inhibiting GlyT2 raises synaptic glycine to restore it. GlyT2 knockouts are lethal but knockdowns are analgesic, so the therapeutic window is partial inhibition. N-arachidonyl glycine was a weak, unstable, promiscuous lead; swapping to an oleoyl tail and D-amino-acid heads gave oleoyl-D-lysine (IC₅₀ ~50 nM), stable in liver and plasma, with only weak off-target mu-opioid activity. ORG25543 is potent but pseudo-irreversible, so prolonged inhibition prevents vesicle reloading and causes on-target side effects (seizures). RPI-GLYT2-82 is ~50-fold less potent, non-competitive, allosteric and fully reversible within 5 minutes, and cryo-EM and molecular dynamics guide further changes to its R groups.',
