              'label': 'Absorption: passive diffusion, influx and efflux transporters',
              'images': [],
              'description': 'Drugs cross the intestinal wall mainly by passive diffusion down a concentration gradient, helped by SLC influx transporters and opposed by ABC efflux transporters such as P-glycoprotein. Ionisation of weak acids and bases, set by pH and pKa, controls how readily they diffuse.',
              'mechanism': 'Passive flux follows Fick\'s law: flux = PA(C1 − C2), so high permeability, large surface area, a steep gradient and a thin membrane all favour absorption. SLC transporters (OATs, OATPs, OCTs, OCTNs) do not hydrolyse ATP but can act as secondary active transporters and generally increase uptake. ABC efflux transporters such as P-gp (MDR1, gene ABCB1) in enterocytes pump drug back into the gut lumen and lower absorption; P-gp knockout mice absorb more oral paclitaxel. Only the un-ionised form of a weak acid or base diffuses readily, and the Henderson–Hasselbalch equation gives the ionised:un-ionised ratio from pH − pKa. The same factors govern entry of drug into target cells.',
              'funFacts': [],
              'notes': [],
              'activeRecall': ['Write Fick\'s law for passive flux and name two changes that increase it.', 'Why does knocking out P-glycoprotein raise plasma levels of oral paclitaxel?'],
              'saq': [],
