              'images': [],
              'description': 'Beyond CYPs, variants in drug transporters such as OATP1B1 (gene SLCO1B1) and in HLA genes change drug exposure and hypersensitivity risk. The frequency of these variants differs between geographical ancestries.',
              'mechanism': 'OATP1B1 is a hepatic influx transporter for statins. The SLCO1B1 521T>C reduced-function variant lowers liver uptake, raising plasma statin levels and the risk of side effects (which can harm adherence), and may reduce efficacy because the target is in the liver. HLA genes encode MHC proteins that present peptides to T-cell receptors; some variants predict hypersensitivity, such as HLA-B*57:01 with abacavir, HLA-B*15:02 with carbamazepine SJS/TEN in Han Chinese, and HLA-B*58:01 with allopurinol. Pre-prescription HLA-B*57:01 screening cut abacavir hypersensitivity sharply. SNPs in coding regions change the protein, regulatory-region SNPs change the amount of mRNA and protein, and intron/exon boundary SNPs disturb splicing. CYP2D6 ultrarapid metabolisers are most common in Middle-eastern populations.',
