              'label': 'The drug development pipeline and regulation',
              'images': [],
              'description': 'Bringing a new drug to market passes through discovery, development and regulation. Only about 1 in 1 million compounds becomes an approved drug.',
              'mechanism': 'Discovery means understanding the drug target and indication and finding lead molecules from natural products (e.g. morphine from the poppy) or by ligand- or structure-based design. Ligand-based design starts from a known ligand such as adrenaline and changes functional groups (adrenaline to the selective β2 agonist salbutamol), while structure-based design starts from the target itself, such as the β2 adrenoceptor binding pocket (salmeterol, formoterol, indacaterol). Both use combinatorial libraries, in silico binding analysis and quantitative structure-activity models, then cell testing. Because receptor subtypes are located in specific tissues, selectivity matters. Development moves from in silico and in vitro work to tissues, whole animals and then patients, with pre-clinical pharmacology (PD and PK), pre-clinical toxicology and clinical trials. Regulation in Australia is registration by the TGA (can be prescribed) and reimbursement through the PBS (subsidised by the Government).',
