              'images': [],
              'description': 'An adverse drug reaction (ADR) is a response to a medicine that is noxious and unintended. Preclinical toxicology uses in vitro and animal studies, designed to ICH guidelines, to find ADRs before a drug reaches people.',
              'mechanism': 'Type A (augmented) ADRs are dose-related exaggerations of the drug\'s mechanism of action; Type B (bizarre) reactions such as allergy and anaphylaxis are unrelated to the mechanism and less common; Types C (chronic), D (delayed) and E (end of use, on withdrawal) extend this. Acute single-dose studies in two mammalian species (one non-rodent) by two routes inform overdose (Type A) toxicity and give IV PK data, while repeat-dose studies detect chronic effects up to the maximum tolerated dose. The safety core battery covers cardiovascular, CNS and respiratory systems; reproductive studies look for teratogenicity across F0 and F1 generations, a lesson from thalidomide, which mice were less sensitive to than rabbits and primates. Genotoxicity is tested with the Ames bacterial mutation test (strains differ in sensitivity), cytogenetic tests and the in vivo micronucleus assay, and a 2-year rodent study compares tumour rates against zero-dose controls. A drug only progresses to clinical trials if its ADRs are reversible, preventable or treatable.',
