              'mechanism': 'Phase I is an open-label, single-arm study in about 100 (sometimes fewer) healthy volunteers or patients; it tests multiple doses to find a safe dose range and uses PK to set the route and dosing interval, and about 50% of drugs stop here. Phase II involves up to 1000 patients in mostly double-blind, placebo-controlled trials of usually two doses to confirm efficacy and common ADRs; about a third pass. Phase III enrols 1,000 to 10,000 people with a defined condition in double-blind randomised trials against the best existing drug or placebo; about a quarter reach approval. Randomisation reduces bias and makes groups similar at baseline so the intervention is the main difference. After registration (TGA in Australia) and PBS listing decided by the PBAC on cost-effectiveness, Phase IV follows over 10,000 unselected people in the community, relying on voluntary reporting, and often first reveals rarer ADRs.',
